eporting, daily market roundups, interviews with prominent industry figures, comprehensive coverage (often exclusive) of important industry events and analyses of market-affecting developments.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"},"audioTts":null,"createdAt":"2026-08-14T08:30:07-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T09:29:47-0400","image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/a913f0f0-f21a-4950-be60-d1581c822e01.png?top=0.15981657608696\u0026left=0\u0026width=1672\u0026height=940.68036684783\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=9d4a67046e98ee31eb5ea7d06bbdecd32665db18d2e37d63ffc1753d8082c31e"}}},"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":231298,"title":"Cooler CPI and PPI boost gold and ease rate hike bets, but the Fed needs more from core ahead of September – Experts  ","__typename":"NewsArticle","category":{"id":47,"name":"Economy","urlAlias":"/news/category/economy"},"teaserSnippet":"(Kitco News) – Gold prices got a boost from a reassuringly in-line CPI report on Wednesday, and while Thursday morning’s equally benign PPI print didn’t push prices higher, the yellow metal has successfully broken out of its recent range and is holding above $4,350 on Thursday afternoon, exactly $100 below its post-CPI peak.\u0026nbsp;","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/article/2026-08-13/cooler-cpi-and-ppi-boost-gold-and-ease-rate-hike-bets-fed-needs-more-core","source":{"name":"Kitco News","subtitle":"The Leading News Source in Precious Metals","description":"\u003cp\u003eKitco NEWS has a diverse team of journalists reporting on the economy, stock markets, commodities, cryptocurrencies, mining and metals with accuracy and objectivity. Our goal is to help people make informed market decisions through in-depth reporting, daily market roundups, interviews with prominent industry figures, comprehensive coverage (often exclusive) of important industry events and analyses of market-affecting developments.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"},"audioTts":null,"createdAt":"2026-08-13T15:12:20-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-13T15:29:30-0400","image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/1758e363-f3c7-4458-9b3b-b7621d750b11.png?top=0.15981657608696\u0026left=0\u0026width=1672\u0026height=940.68036684783\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=0e0f8fa17dbdcc3a830fd5aa740b02b9adf758323477870caae4cea18283d1ad"}}},"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":231280,"title":"UBS sees gold price challenging $5,000/oz in H1 2027 on lower real rates, softer USD and strong sovereign demand ","__typename":"NewsArticle","category":{"id":48,"name":"Gold","urlAlias":"/news/category/commodities/gold"},"teaserSnippet":"(Kitco News) – Gold prices have faced the strong headwind of a high opportunity cost for much of 2026, but falling real rates will drive investors back to the precious metal, with a weaker dollar and strong central bank demand helping to propel prices back toward $5,000 per ounce in the first half of 2027, according to strategists at UBS.","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/article/2026-08-13/ubs-sees-gold-price-challenging-5000oz-h1-2027-lower-real-rates-softer-usd","source":{"name":"Kitco News","subtitle":"The Leading News Source in Precious Metals","description":"\u003cp\u003eKitco NEWS has a diverse team of journalists reporting on the economy, stock markets, commodities, cryptocurrencies, mining and metals with accuracy and objectivity. Our goal is to help people make informed market decisions through in-depth reporting, daily market roundups, interviews with prominent industry figures, comprehensive coverage (often exclusive) of important industry events and analyses of market-affecting developments.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"},"audioTts":null,"createdAt":"2026-08-13T10:43:34-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-13T11:28:59-0400","image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/ef0f07bb-25f9-4f8d-86ee-dcb1d6391080.png?top=0.15981657608696\u0026left=0\u0026width=1672\u0026height=940.68036684783\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=00d11bea0ab4ae582fdcbbf2425e87e8b49523e4397ef887ff1a2253c52cc1fe"}}},"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":231249,"title":"Spot gold approaches $4,400/oz after U.S. weekly jobless claims rise to 209k","__typename":"NewsArticle","category":{"id":47,"name":"Economy","urlAlias":"/news/category/economy"},"teaserSnippet":"(Kitco News) -\u0026nbsp;Gold prices are getting closer to the $4,400 per ounce level on Thursday morning following the release of\u0026nbsp;worse than expected\u0026nbsp;labor market data after the number of Americans filing new claims for unemployment benefits came in\u0026nbsp;above\u0026nbsp;economists’ forecasts.","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/article/2026-08-13/spot-gold-approaches-4400oz-after-us-weekly-jobless-claims-rise-209k","source":{"name":"Kitco News","subtitle":"The Leading News Source in Precious Metals","description":"\u003cp\u003eKitco NEWS has a diverse team of journalists reporting on the economy, stock markets, commodities, cryptocurrencies, mining and metals with accuracy and objectivity. Our goal is to help people make informed market decisions through in-depth reporting, daily market roundups, interviews with prominent industry figures, comprehensive coverage (often exclusive) of important industry events and analyses of market-affecting developments.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"},"audioTts":null,"createdAt":"2026-08-13T08:30:10-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-13T08:52:02-0400","image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/f4ab4a7f-78c8-4293-be58-927febd605b8.jpeg?top=0.12805706521739\u0026left=0\u0026width=1340\u0026height=753.74388586957\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=536aa8b82b8ffa97dac39db38276603a31debf5f618667ef5ee9f6461fb78bd4"}}},"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":231243,"title":"Spot gold at $4,387/oz after July PPI posts flat 0.0%, core PPI up 0.2%","__typename":"NewsArticle","category":{"id":47,"name":"Economy","urlAlias":"/news/category/economy"},"teaserSnippet":"(Kitco News) - The\u0026nbsp;gold market is holding steady after the latest data shows U.S. producers saw\u0026nbsp;easing\u0026nbsp;price pressures on balance last month.The headline Producer Price Index (PPI) posted a flat 0.0% in July, following June’s unrevised -0.3% drop, the U.S. Labor Department announced on Thursday. The latest headline data was lower than expectations, as economists looked for a 0.2% increase.","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/article/2026-08-13/spot-gold-4387oz-after-july-ppi-posts-flat-00-core-ppi-02","source":{"name":"Kitco News","subtitle":"The Leading News Source in Precious Metals","description":"\u003cp\u003eKitco NEWS has a diverse team of journalists reporting on the economy, stock markets, commodities, cryptocurrencies, mining and metals with accuracy and objectivity. Our goal is to help people make informed market decisions through in-depth reporting, daily market roundups, interviews with prominent industry figures, comprehensive coverage (often exclusive) of important industry events and analyses of market-affecting developments.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"},"audioTts":null,"createdAt":"2026-08-13T08:30:12-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-13T08:43:45-0400","image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/93f7eb05-0c62-4d9e-8cbc-97bbd957b987.png?top=0.15981657608696\u0026left=0\u0026width=1672\u0026height=940.68036684783\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=b7c91d350ce360ee1545ca520be19a095f5d956f7d4937efb3de68d12bae70e6"}}},"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":231184,"title":"LBMA snapshot survey predicts gold price average near $4,500/oz by year-end","__typename":"NewsArticle","category":{"id":48,"name":"Gold","urlAlias":"/news/category/commodities/gold"},"teaserSnippet":"(Kitco News) – Spot gold will trade on either side of $4,500 per ounce at the end of 2026, according to a new survey published by the London Bullion Market Association (LBMA).The LBMA surveyed 16 professional analysts in July, and even as gold was trading at its 2026 lows, frequently dipping below $4,000 per ounce during the month, the average of the experts’ year-end price predictions was over 12% higher.","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/article/2026-08-12/lbma-snapshot-survey-predicts-gold-price-average-near-4500oz-year-end","source":{"name":"Kitco News","subtitle":"The Leading News Source in Precious Metals","description":"\u003cp\u003eKitco NEWS has a diverse team of journalists reporting on the economy, stock markets, commodities, cryptocurrencies, mining and metals with accuracy and objectivity. Our goal is to help people make informed market decisions through in-depth reporting, daily market roundups, interviews with prominent industry figures, comprehensive coverage (often exclusive) of important industry events and analyses of market-affecting developments.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"},"audioTts":null,"createdAt":"2026-08-12T13:26:20-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-12T13:37:31-0400","image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/7cb8e990-96a6-4e2d-ada6-d1a174b6ffaa.png?top=0.15981657608696\u0026left=0\u0026width=1672\u0026height=940.68036684783\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=a1801e50f0548a8d9dd142411c2079a21241a9aa3b80e0f25c6f84571b5e2328"}}},"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":231181,"title":"Fed is still ‘inflation-first’ after cooler CPI, but weak jobs report means it’s no longer ‘inflation-only’ – Natixis","__typename":"NewsArticle","category":{"id":47,"name":"Economy","urlAlias":"/news/category/economy"},"teaserSnippet":"(Kitco News) – Wednesday morning’s CPI report is proof that broad inflationary pressures are easing, but while the Federal Reserve is likely to remain focused on inflation between now and the September meeting, last Friday’s dismal nonfarm payrolls data means both sides of the dual mandate are back in play, according to economists at Natixis.Economists Christopher Hodge and Selin Aker wrote that last week’s employment report casts the in-line CPI data in a different light.","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/article/2026-08-12/fed-still-inflation-first-after-cooler-cpi-weak-jobs-report-means-its-no","source":{"name":"Kitco News","subtitle":"The Leading News Source in Precious Metals","description":"\u003cp\u003eKitco NEWS has a diverse team of journalists reporting on the economy, stock markets, commodities, cryptocurrencies, mining and metals with accuracy and objectivity. Our goal is to help people make informed market decisions through in-depth reporting, daily market roundups, interviews with prominent industry figures, comprehensive coverage (often exclusive) of important industry events and analyses of market-affecting developments.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"},"audioTts":null,"createdAt":"2026-08-12T11:52:53-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-12T12:31:46-0400","image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/07fc3bca-6878-403c-a6b2-47e5f5bab984.png?top=0.15981657608696\u0026left=0\u0026width=1672\u0026height=940.68036684783\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=54382e2b94e1a37ad7c38551f048cd0e0c3223d88087d4e9de15381d4200c1c9"}}},"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":231199,"title":"In-line CPI clears the path to $4,600","__typename":"Commentary","category":{"id":114,"name":"Opinion","urlAlias":"/opinion"},"teaserSnippet":"(Kitco Commentary) - Today gold's continued upside potential was realized, made possible by a CPI report that came in exactly in line with economists' expectations. ","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/opinion/2026-08-12/line-cpi-clears-path-4600","source":{"name":"Kitco Commentaries","subtitle":"Opinions, Ideas and Markets Talk","description":"\u003cp\u003eFeaturing views and opinions written by market professionals, not staff journalists.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"},"audioTts":null,"createdAt":"2026-08-12T18:44:21-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-12T18:48:20-0400","image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/3c88f981-8ccc-4158-a411-233f6901ea67.jpeg?top=0\u0026left=3.7491479209271\u0026width=992.50170415815\u0026height=558\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=e314144f0f52ad267bd9a66558bd9fd0d70bc2f296dcd70863214e8db8e709bf"}}},"teaserImage":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":null}}},"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":231378,"title":"Wall Street goes full-bull on gold as rate-hike bets recede, Main Street maintains bullish majority with Fed minutes on the menu ","__typename":"NewsArticle","category":{"id":48,"name":"Gold","urlAlias":"/news/category/commodities/gold"},"teaserSnippet":"(Kitco News) – Gold prices pushed higher this week, as fading expectations for a September Fed rate hike helped the precious metal extend its August rebound despite intermittent pressure from a firmer dollar, rising oil prices, and profit-taking.","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/article/2026-08-14/wall-street-goes-full-bull-gold-rate-hike-bets-recede-main-street-maintains","source":{"name":"Kitco News","subtitle":"The Leading News Source in Precious Metals","description":"\u003cp\u003eKitco NEWS has a diverse team of journalists reporting on the economy, stock markets, commodities, cryptocurrencies, mining and metals with accuracy and objectivity. Our goal is to help people make informed market decisions through in-depth reporting, daily market roundups, interviews with prominent industry figures, comprehensive coverage (often exclusive) of important industry events and analyses of market-affecting developments.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"},"audioTts":null,"createdAt":"2026-08-14T17:58:44-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T18:25:12-0400","image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/b9094838-3afd-4004-aeb5-ca68e554e806.png?top=0.15981657608696\u0026left=0\u0026width=1672\u0026height=940.68036684783\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=e508b2cda35aa564225b8a6b4634ac07e006c77984e774bc9856011a23b32d41"}}},"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":231364,"title":"Canadian dollar heads for third straight weekly gain as yield spreads narrow","__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eTORONTO, Aug 14 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar strengthened to a two-month high against its U.S. counterpart on Friday as domestic factory data supported recent ​narrowing in the gap between U.S. and Canadian bond yields.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe loonie ‌was trading 0.4% higher at 1.3875 per U.S. dollar, or 72.07 U.S. cents, after touching its strongest intraday level since June 3 at 1.3865. For the week, the ​currency was up 0.5%, putting it on track for its third ​straight weekly gain.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\"The retrenchment in Fed tightening expectations — which has ⁠further to go, we believe — has driven a significant narrowing in front-end ​spreads since the end of July,\" Shaun Osborne and Eric Theoret, strategists at ​Scotiabank, said in a note.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe gap between Canada's 2-year yield and the U.S. equivalent has narrowed by about 17 basis points this month to 120 basis points in favor ​of the U.S. note.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"At the margin, Friday's disappointing U.S. retail sales data ​and the better-than-expected Canadian manufacturing sales data sustain the recent shift in the trend of ‌relative ⁠data surprises ... which will also feed through to the exchange rate,\" the strategists said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eCanadian factory sales grew 0.1% in June from May, the fifth straight month of gains, while sales volumes were up 1.2%.\u003cbr\u003eSeparate data showed wholesale trade ​rising by 2.8% ​in June.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe U.S. dollar ⁠fell against a basket of major currencies after data showed U.S. retail sales unexpectedly declined in July.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe price of oil , ​one of Canada's major exports, was trading 0.8% higher ​at $81.88 a ⁠barrel after the U.S. threatened an indefinite naval blockade of Iran, raising concerns about disruptions to crude supplies from the Middle East.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eCanadian government bond yields moved ⁠higher across ​a steeper curve, tracking moves in U.S. ​Treasuries. The 10-year was up 5.5 basis points at 3.681%, but holding below the two-year high ​it touched on Tuesday at 3.755%.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eReporting by Fergal Smith; Editing by Paul Simao\u003c/p\u003e","category":null,"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-08-14/canadian-dollar-heads-third-straight-weekly-gain-yield-spreads-narrow","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-08-14T13:05:39-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T13:06:31-0400","imageUrl":null,"featured":true,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/a1b24c45-3549-4749-8ff8-49b0b966f967.webp?top=0\u0026left=0.14542149511479\u0026width=1279.7091570098\u0026height=720\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=5de41b27b0e172281fa5d3b629b3f413628fec9b929b81979102f4971c92f628"}}},"audioTts":null,"teaserSnippet":"TORONTO, Aug 14 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar strengthened to a two-month high against its U.S. counterpart on Friday as domestic factory data supported recent ​narrowing in the gap between U.S. and Canadian bond yields."},{"id":231360,"title":"Wall St subdued as markets pause after S\u0026P record high; data, Mideast in focus","__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eAug 14 (Reuters) - Wall Street's main ​indexes were largely muted in choppy trading on Friday, a day after the S\u0026amp;P 500 closed at a ‌record high, while investors assessed retail sales data and took stock of the latest developments in the Middle East.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eGains in Meta Platforms (META.O), and Alphabet (GOOGL.O), lifted the S\u0026amp;P 500 communication services index (.SPLRCL), 0.5%, but mixed moves in chip stocks such as Broadcom (AVGO.O), and Nvidia (NVDA.O), left the S\u0026amp;P 500 and the ​Nasdaq swinging between gains and losses.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eApplied Materials (AMAT.O), fell 4%, even after the chip equipment maker forecast fourth-quarter revenue above Wall Street ​estimates.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eEnergy stocks on the S\u0026amp;P 500 (.SPNY), rose 1.6%, while miners (.SPLRCM), and industrials (.SPLRCI), gained about 0.5% each.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"It's ⁠good to see an expansion in the markets here with broadening out, not just hiding out in hyperscalers like Microsoft and ​Nvidia,\" said Bob Lang, founder and chief strategist at Explosive Options.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eDeclines in Amgen (AMGN.O), and Johnson \u0026amp; Johnson (JNJ.N), weighed on the Dow.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAt 10:05 ​a.m. ET, the Dow Jones Industrial Average (.DJI), fell 21.14 points, or 0.04%, to 53,818.85, the S\u0026amp;P 500 (.SPX), gained 8.66 points, or 0.11%, to 7,807.65 and the Nasdaq Composite (.IXIC), gained 17.78 points, or 0.07%, to 26,822.61.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eInvestors also parsed a weaker-than-expected retail sales reading for July, after an unrevised 0.2% gain in June, ​the Commerce Department's Census Bureau said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"If they (the Fed) raise rates at the next meeting, it's not going to be a horrible ​thing ... If they do not change policy, it means that they have confidence that inflation trends are moving in the right direction,\" said Lang.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e‌The figures ⁠follow benign inflation data earlier this week that eased some concerns about a Federal Reserve interest-rate hike in September, putting the S\u0026amp;P 500 and the Nasdaq on track for a third consecutive weekly gain, their longest winning streak since early April.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe University of Michigan's preliminary consumer sentiment survey came in at 51 in August, below expectations of 54.5, according to economists polled by Reuters.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eDevelopments in the Middle ​East continued to weigh on ​investor sentiment after transit through ⁠the Strait of Hormuz appeared to have come to a near standstill after two more ships were attacked and the U.S. said it could maintain a naval blockade of Iran indefinitely.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmong other stocks, ​Workday (WDAY.O), slipped 4.4%. Reuters reported on Thursday that private equity firm Silver Lake was in talks ​to acquire the ⁠software firm.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eReddit surged nearly 15% after the social media company was named a new addition to the S\u0026amp;P 500 index, effective August 18.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eShares of some drone makers gained after President Donald Trump said late on Thursday that he would impose tariffs on imports of drones and their components.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eRed Cat (RCAT.O), jumped ⁠7% ​and Unusual Machines (UMAC.A), added 14%.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAdvancing issues outnumbered decliners by a 1.45-to-1 ratio on ​the NYSE and by a 1.02-to-1 ratio on the Nasdaq.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe S\u0026amp;P 500 posted nine new 52-week highs and one new low while the Nasdaq Composite recorded ​49 new highs and 32 new lows.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eReporting by Avinash P and Purvi Agarwal in Bengaluru; Editing by Tasim Zahid and Anil D'Silva\u003c/p\u003e","category":null,"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-08-14/wall-st-subdued-markets-pause-after-sp-record-high-data-mideast-focus","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-08-14T12:42:22-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T12:44:22-0400","imageUrl":null,"featured":true,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/a1b24c45-3549-4749-8ff8-49b0b966f967.webp?top=0\u0026left=0.14542149511479\u0026width=1279.7091570098\u0026height=720\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=5de41b27b0e172281fa5d3b629b3f413628fec9b929b81979102f4971c92f628"}}},"audioTts":null,"teaserSnippet":"Aug 14 (Reuters) - Wall Street's main ​indexes were largely muted in choppy trading on Friday, a day after the S\u0026amp;P 500 closed at a ‌record high, while investors assessed retail sales data and took stock of the latest developments in the Middle East.Gains in Meta Platforms (META.O), and Alphabet (GOOGL.O), lifted the S\u0026amp;P 500 communication services index (.SPLRCL), 0.5%, but mixed moves in chip stocks such as Broadcom (AVGO.O), and Nvidia (NVDA.O), left the S\u0026amp;P 500 and the ​Nasdaq swinging between gains and losses."},{"id":231359,"title":"India clears law to curb new state taxes on mining","__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eIndia’s parliament on Thursday passed a bill that will restrict state governments from imposing new taxes, cesses or other levies on mineral rights and mineral-bearing lands unless permitted under conditions set by the federal government.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe bill aims to create a uniform tax framework for the mining sector and address what the federal government described as an excessive and uneven fiscal burden on miners across states.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe current system had led to multiple taxes and levies, differing tax rates among states, and retrospective demands that created uncertainty for mining companies and investors, the federal government said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eUnder the law, the federal government will also gain regulatory oversight of mineral-bearing lands containing mineral resources, expanding its role beyond the existing control over mine regulation and mineral development.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe legislation invalidates unpaid or uncollected levies that states may have sought before the law comes into force.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(Reporting by Mridula Kumar in Bengaluru; Editing by Mrigank Dhaniwala)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-08-14/india-clears-law-curb-new-state-taxes-mining","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-08-14T12:39:28-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T12:41:48-0400","imageUrl":null,"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/0f500ee4-2b5e-477b-8437-9e14d88505ae.webp?top=0\u0026left=0.14542149511479\u0026width=1279.7091570098\u0026height=720\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=d4972e6f7c8e44fffb9dda47fde4da3d3c4722905e0f272d50ffc89b527f362d"}}},"audioTts":null,"teaserSnippet":"India’s parliament on Thursday passed a bill that will restrict state governments from imposing new taxes, cesses or other levies on mineral rights and mineral-bearing lands unless permitted under conditions set by the federal government.The bill aims to create a uniform tax framework for the mining sector and address what the federal government described as an excessive and uneven fiscal burden on miners across states."},{"id":231358,"title":"New Zealand grants new energy mining permit in the Taranaki Basin","__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eNew Zealand’s centre-right coalition government on Thursday granted a new energy mining permit in the onshore Taranaki ​region, an oil-and-gas-rich area on the country’s North Island, ‌saying it would bring more gas to the domestic market.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eMatahio NZ Onshore Ltd was awarded a 10-year permit covering parts of ​the Puka field and the Oru prospect, Resources ​Minister Shane Jones said in a statement.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAs only ⁠the second petroleum mining permit to be granted ​in the past eight years but one of two ​granted in the last year or so, this is yet another signal that momentum in New Zealand’s petroleum sector is growing,” Jones ​said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eJones, an advocate for the fossil fuel sector, ​said gas would remain critical to New Zealand’s energy mix in ‌the ⁠decades ahead, helping support power generation in dry years when hydropower output falls.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eMatahio estimates recoverable reserves from the existing Puka discovery at about 170,000 barrels of oil ​and 1.3 billion ​cubic feet ⁠of gas.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAn independent assessment of the Oru prospect estimates prospective resources of 1.8 million ​barrels of oil and 1.2 billion cubic ​feet ⁠of gas.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe Labour Party-led government in 2018 blocked all new offshore permits and limited onshore permits to existing allocations ⁠in ​Taranaki.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eCritics have blamed the policy for ​contributing to a surge in energy prices as domestic gas production ​declined.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(Reporting by Renju Jose in Sydney; Editing by Lincoln Feast.)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-08-14/new-zealand-grants-new-energy-mining-permit-taranaki-basin","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-08-14T12:36:19-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T12:39:19-0400","imageUrl":null,"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/60716a09-0c27-46ba-b48b-2a42fabcc165.webp?top=0\u0026left=0.14542149511479\u0026width=1279.7091570098\u0026height=720\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=988f370b7146fe9c5a4f67f3527f32ceadf9cc104a6a393dc93da48e609a923a"}}},"audioTts":null,"teaserSnippet":"New Zealand’s centre-right coalition government on Thursday granted a new energy mining permit in the onshore Taranaki ​region, an oil-and-gas-rich area on the country’s North Island, ‌saying it would bring more gas to the domestic market.Matahio NZ Onshore Ltd was awarded a 10-year permit covering parts of ​the Puka field and the Oru prospect, Resources ​Minister Shane Jones said in a statement."},{"id":231357,"title":"STG buys large amount of aluminum from Radiant World, sources say","__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;STG, a trading company funded by principals of the U.S. hedge fund Squarepoint, has bought a large amount of aluminum from Radiant World as the commodity trading house sells off inventory to raise cash, two industry sources said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAsked about the transaction, Radiant said it did not comment on commercial matters. Squarepoint declined to comment.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eRadiant, which is best known as an iron ore trader but also deals in base metals, has come under intense scrutiny in recent weeks because of concerns over the validity of invoices provided to its banks.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eDeutsche Bank and KBC Group have frozen some of Radiant’s Singapore bank accounts, while some other lenders have suspended credit lines, Bloomberg News reported last week. Radiant has declined to comment on any discussions with specific lenders but says it conducts its business to the highest commercial and legal standards.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe exact volume of aluminium sold by Radiant to STG and the price at which the transaction was agreed were not known.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe sources said, however, that STG was buying a substantial amount of the aluminium offered by Radiant.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eOne of the sources said Radiant’s stocks were distributed across multiple locations globally, including China and Europe.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eA third source familiar with the matter estimated Radiant had about 40,000-50,000 metric tons of aluminium in various locations. The material is being held in private storage rather than in exchange warehouses, the source said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSuch a stockpile would be worth between $130 million and $165 million at current aluminium prices CMAL3, although sources expect Radiant would have had to sell the metal at a discount.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eA fourth source familiar with the matter said that in addition to aluminium, Radiant was looking to offload 1,500 tons of copper inventories.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003ePotential buyers are bidding aggressively for the material, the fourth source added.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAll the sources declined to be named because of the sensitivity of the matter.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(Reporting by Amy Lv, Tom Daly and Pratima Desai; Editing by Nia Williams)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-08-14/stg-buys-large-amount-aluminum-radiant-world-sources-say","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-08-14T12:32:34-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T12:35:46-0400","imageUrl":null,"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/d5881efd-3deb-4287-b174-504b87278a7c.webp?top=0\u0026left=0.14542149511479\u0026width=1279.7091570098\u0026height=720\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=ffb0fe9b057f7eb30fe30b3afcf6eae24638153288c102d5809e2dc45836bf54"}}},"audioTts":null,"teaserSnippet":"\u0026nbsp;STG, a trading company funded by principals of the U.S. hedge fund Squarepoint, has bought a large amount of aluminum from Radiant World as the commodity trading house sells off inventory to raise cash, two industry sources said.Asked about the transaction, Radiant said it did not comment on commercial matters. Squarepoint declined to comment.Radiant, which is best known as an iron ore trader but also deals in base metals, has come under intense scrutiny in recent weeks because of concerns over the validity of invoices provided to its banks."},{"id":231356,"title":"India’s Jharkhand state CM urges Modi to rethink mining bill, citing revenue hit","__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eThe chief minister of the Indian state of Jharkhand has urged Prime Minister Narendra Modi to reconsider a bill that would restrict states’ ability to levy charges on mining activities.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIn an August 13 letter to Modi posted on X, CM Hemant Soren said the state’s Mineral Bearing Land Cess was expected to generate about 71.1 billion rupees ($744.97 million) a year and that mining-related revenue was critical to the eastern state’s finances.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSoren said that mining revenue accounted for 84.9% of Jharkhand’s own non-tax revenue in fiscal year 2024-25.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e“Any substantial restriction on this revenue would directly affect Jharkhand’s ability to sustain development, welfare and social-security interventions,” Soren said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIndia’s parliament on Thursday passed a bill that will restrict state ​governments from imposing new taxes ‌or other levies on mineral rights and mineral-bearing land unless permitted under conditions ​set by the federal government.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe bill aims to ⁠create a uniform tax framework ​for the mining sector and address what ​the federal government says is excessive and uneven fiscal burden on miners across states.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe fallout could go beyond government finances, Soren said, raising public discontent and social tensions in communities that have long borne the costs of mining.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e($1 = 95.4400 Indian rupees)\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(Reporting by Jatindra Dash and Abhinav Parmar; Editing by Sonia Cheema)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-08-14/indias-jharkhand-state-cm-urges-modi-rethink-mining-bill-citing","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-08-14T12:29:07-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T12:32:27-0400","imageUrl":null,"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/c8114b56-8e1c-468c-b2a0-eac6e08bd1b4.webp?top=0\u0026left=0.14542149511479\u0026width=1279.7091570098\u0026height=720\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=cce5ed46100586a4bf785eb09227952b4dfe80fcaa5312124c7a444d0a734f68"}}},"audioTts":null,"teaserSnippet":"The chief minister of the Indian state of Jharkhand has urged Prime Minister Narendra Modi to reconsider a bill that would restrict states’ ability to levy charges on mining activities.In an August 13 letter to Modi posted on X, CM Hemant Soren said the state’s Mineral Bearing Land Cess was expected to generate about 71.1 billion rupees ($744.97 million) a year and that mining-related revenue was critical to the eastern state’s finances.Soren said that mining revenue accounted for 84.9% of Jharkhand’s own non-tax revenue in fiscal year 2024-25."},{"id":231355,"title":"Norsk Hydro’s Alunorte ramps up production after deal with gas supplier","__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eNorsk Hydro’s NHY.OL Alunorte refinery in Brazil has started ramping up alumina production to full capacity after reaching a temporary agreement with its gas supplier, the Norwegian company said on Thursday.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe refinery, located in Brazil’s northern state of Para, had reduced output to 50% of capacity earlier this week after gas supplier CELBA informed about disruptions in availability. It has the capacity to produce 6.3 million metric tons of alumina per year.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe cut exacerbated concerns over global aluminium supplies already strained by constricted Middle East shipments and low inventories in LME warehouses, driving prices to their highest in seven weeks.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe production loss is estimated to be 100,000 to 120,000 tonnes, Hydro said, adding that Alunorte has reached an agreement with CELBA regarding temporary terminal access.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe company has also recently received approval from Brazil’s oil regulator to become a self-importer of gas and is pursuing a long-term solution for its gas supply, it said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe Iran war has caused gas prices to spike, while efforts to reopen the Strait of Hormuz, which carried a fifth of global oil and liquefied natural gas shipments before its access was blocked, remain complicated.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe reduced production and purchasing gas above the contract price would result in a potential financial impact of $75 million to $100 million, Hydro said.​\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e(Reporting by Abu Sultan in Bengaluru and Kanjyik Ghosh in Barcelona; Editing by Shilpi Majumdar)\u003c/p\u003e","category":{"id":56,"name":"Mining","urlAlias":"/news/category/mining"},"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-08-14/norsk-hydros-alunorte-ramps-production-after-deal-gas-supplier","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-08-14T12:07:10-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T12:20:22-0400","imageUrl":null,"featured":false,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/281348fb-a514-408a-b890-453b992de0b9.webp?top=0\u0026left=0.14542149511479\u0026width=1279.7091570098\u0026height=720\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=efefa1d8e04e37a8df179ce5b4958023f01cbf6b435d4d640ec3f3f61804f896"}}},"audioTts":null,"teaserSnippet":"Norsk Hydro’s NHY.OL Alunorte refinery in Brazil has started ramping up alumina production to full capacity after reaching a temporary agreement with its gas supplier, the Norwegian company said on Thursday.The refinery, located in Brazil’s northern state of Para, had reduced output to 50% of capacity earlier this week after gas supplier CELBA informed about disruptions in availability. It has the capacity to produce 6.3 million metric tons of alumina per year."},{"id":231348,"title":"TSX on track for sixth straight day of gains as miners rally","__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eAugust 14 (Reuters) - Canada's commodity-heavy stock index notched a ​fresh intraday high on Friday, lifted by miners as gold advanced ‌on a weaker dollar, while investors also assessed corporate earnings and stalled talks to end the Middle East conflict.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe S\u0026amp;P/TSX Composite Index (.GSPTSE), rose 0.1% to 36,813.74 points by 10:12 a.m., after ​hitting an intraday record of 36,844.73 points earlier in the day. ​The benchmark was on track for its sixth straight day of ⁠gains — its longest daily winning streak since April.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe materials (.GSPTTMT), index, which includes metal ​miners, was up 1.9%, tracking gains in precious metals. Miners Seabridge Gold (SEA.TO), Endeavour Silver (EDR.TO), ​and First Majestic Silver (AG.TO), advanced more than 4% each.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eHeavyweight energy shares (.SPTTEN), added 1.1% as crude oil rose after the U.S. threatened an indefinite naval blockade of Iran, raising concerns about disruptions ​to global oil supplies.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eBird Construction's (BDT.TO), shares jumped 6.3% after the company beat second-quarter ​profit estimates, prompting at least three brokerages to raise their price targets on the stock.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"Investors ‌have been ⁠focusing on earnings growth, and we've had pretty robust earnings growth,\" said Michael Dehal, a senior portfolio manager at Dehal Investment Partners at Raymond James.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eTechnology shares (.SPTTTK), which have gained more than 11% quarter-to-date, shed 1.9%.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eOf the 10 major sectors on ​the TSX, only energy ​and materials were ⁠in the green.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIn company news, Air Canada's (AC.TO), revenue for September and October is likely to break records for the two ​months, a senior executive said on Friday. Its shares were ​little changed.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eDomestic ⁠wholesale trade grew by 2.8% in June from May driven by higher sales in the machinery, equipment and supplies sub-sector, Statistics Canada said on Friday.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSeparately, a Canadian government source ⁠directly ​familiar with trade negotiations with the U.S. said ​on Thursday talks were progressing well and that Washington also wanted an agreement before a new U.S. ​tariff deadline on August 19.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eReporting by Sudeshna Ghoshal in Bengaluru; Editing by Jonathan Ananda\u003c/p\u003e","category":null,"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-08-14/tsx-track-sixth-straight-day-gains-miners-rally","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-08-14T11:07:46-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T11:09:27-0400","imageUrl":null,"featured":true,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/a8c61b15-b8e3-48e2-a4b5-09fed9190dc1.webp?top=0\u0026left=0.14542149511479\u0026width=1279.7091570098\u0026height=720\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=91764e6e67d095dd339b340181578c3f7d64f136591fdbbda6caf237d9c9446c"}}},"audioTts":null,"teaserSnippet":"August 14 (Reuters) - Canada's commodity-heavy stock index notched a ​fresh intraday high on Friday, lifted by miners as gold advanced ‌on a weaker dollar, while investors also assessed corporate earnings and stalled talks to end the Middle East conflict.The S\u0026amp;P/TSX Composite Index (.GSPTSE), rose 0.1% to 36,813.74 points by 10:12 a.m., after ​hitting an intraday record of 36,844.73 points earlier in the day. ​The benchmark was on track for its sixth straight day of ⁠gains — its longest daily winning streak since April."},{"id":231347,"title":"Gold firms on weaker dollar as inflation data cements rate hold bets","__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eAug 14 (Reuters) - Gold advanced on ‌Friday, buoyed by a weaker dollar after in-line U.S. inflation readings this week tipped the scales in favor of an interest rate hold by the Federal Reserve next month.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSpot gold gained 0.6% to $4,376.02 per ​ounce by 9:09 a.m. EDT (1309 GMT). Prices declined 1.3% in the previous session ​on profit-taking after touching their highest level since June 5. Bullion is ⁠up around 0.7% for the week so far.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eU.S. gold futures rose 0.3% to $4,433.90.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"The lower U.S. dollar ​index is a friendly outside market that's supporting gold today,\" said Jim Wyckoff, a market ​analyst at American Gold Exchange.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe U.S. dollar index slipped 0.3%, making greenback-priced bullion more affordable for buyers overseas.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eGold drew support from an unexpected decline in U.S. nonfarm payrolls in July, and inflation readings this week that ​were largely in line with expectations. This significantly lowered expectations of an increase in interest ​rates next month, with most analysts now seeing the central bank holding the current 3.50% to 3.75% ‌range.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eMarkets ⁠are pricing in a 31% chance of a rate hike at the September meeting, down from about 55% last week, according to CME's FedWatch Tool. FEDWATCH\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"As we expect the Fed not to raise interest rates, gold price therefore still has further upside potential,\" Commerzbank said in ​a note.\u003cbr\u003eLower interest rates ​tend to support ⁠non-yielding gold.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eMeanwhile, transit through the Strait of Hormuz appeared to grind to a near-standstill after two more ships were attacked and the United ​States said it could maintain a naval blockade of Iran indefinitely.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eOil ​prices were on ⁠track for weekly gains on the news.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"If crude oil prices continue to rise, that's going to be a bearish element for the metals market because it would aid inflation and prompt central banks ⁠to ​raise interest rates,\" Wyckoff said.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eElsewhere, gold discounts in India widened ​to more than two-month highs.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSpot silver climbed 1% to $65.08 per ounce, platinum gained 0.9% to $1,733.34, and palladium rose 0.9% ​to $1,318.50.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eReporting by Sukanya Mitra in Bengaluru, additional reporting by Anjana Anil; Editing by Joyjeet Das\u003c/p\u003e","category":null,"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-08-14/gold-firms-weaker-dollar-inflation-data-cements-rate-hold-bets","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-08-14T11:03:49-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T11:04:59-0400","imageUrl":null,"featured":true,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/852274e0-dbf7-4f10-ad77-fb757d6893c9.webp?top=0\u0026left=0.14542149511479\u0026width=1279.7091570098\u0026height=720\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=fcfd27c8ccd6076edf6a42d3cf47bbf751e4ba1976d1d27d30479dac1009ec98"}}},"audioTts":null,"teaserSnippet":"Aug 14 (Reuters) - Gold advanced on ‌Friday, buoyed by a weaker dollar after in-line U.S. inflation readings this week tipped the scales in favor of an interest rate hold by the Federal Reserve next month.Spot gold gained 0.6% to $4,376.02 per ​ounce by 9:09 a.m. EDT (1309 GMT). Prices declined 1.3% in the previous session ​on profit-taking after touching their highest level since June 5. Bullion is ⁠up around 0.7% for the week so far.U.S. gold futures rose 0.3% to $4,433.90."},{"id":231346,"title":"Dollar falls on surprise drop in US retail sales","__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eNEW YORK, Aug 14 (Reuters) - The dollar fell ‌on Friday after data showed U.S. retail sales unexpectedly declined in July, as traders weighed Federal Reserve policy and the prospect of a Bank of Japan rate hike next month aimed at supporting the yen.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eRetail sales dropped 0.6% last month ​after an unrevised 0.2% gain in June. Economists polled by Reuters had forecast retail sales, ​which are mostly goods and are not adjusted for inflation, edging up 0.1%.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003e“We ⁠are clearly having signs of poor consumption,” said Juan Perez, director of trading at Monex USA ​in Washington. “This evidence is clearly showing that there is an economic slowdown in the United States.”\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSofter-than-expected consumer and producer ​price inflation data this week has already tempered expectations that the Fed will raise rates at its September 15-16 meeting. Traders are now pricing in just a 31% probability of a September hike, alongside a 64% chance of ​a rate increase by December.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eConcerns over the labor market have deepened as well, after July's payrolls ​report showed employers unexpectedly shed jobs last month.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThe dollar index , which measures the greenback against a basket of currencies including ‌the ⁠yen and the euro, fell 0.33% to 99.59, with the euro up 0.36% at $1.1568.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe Japanese yen strengthened 0.32% against the greenback to 158.97 per dollar.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eDespite Friday's gains, the yen was on track for a weekly decline of around 0.7% as the effects of recent U.S. and Japanese intervention continued to ​fade. That has left ​traders betting that either ⁠a rate hike or another round of official buying will be needed to arrest the currency's slide.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eReuters reported the Bank of Japan is set to raise ​rates as soon as September and is considering more aggressive hikes to ​follow. Since ⁠exiting a massive, decade-long stimulus in 2024, the BOJ has raised interest rates at a pace of roughly twice a year.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe yen was trading at 40-year lows near 164 per dollar before July's intervention, and ⁠traders ​see the 160 level as a potential trigger for fresh ​official action. Its retreat mirrors a similar selloff in May, when it also fell back after a round of official buying.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eReporting ​by Karen Brettell; Additional reporting by Ankur Banerjee and Reuters' Toyko markets team; Editing by Joe Bavier\u003c/p\u003e","category":null,"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-08-14/dollar-falls-surprise-drop-us-retail-sales","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-08-14T11:02:00-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T11:03:43-0400","imageUrl":null,"featured":true,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/a1b24c45-3549-4749-8ff8-49b0b966f967.webp?top=0\u0026left=0.14542149511479\u0026width=1279.7091570098\u0026height=720\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=5de41b27b0e172281fa5d3b629b3f413628fec9b929b81979102f4971c92f628"}}},"audioTts":null,"teaserSnippet":"NEW YORK, Aug 14 (Reuters) - The dollar fell ‌on Friday after data showed U.S. retail sales unexpectedly declined in July, as traders weighed Federal Reserve policy and the prospect of a Bank of Japan rate hike next month aimed at supporting the yen."},{"id":231337,"title":"Gold steadies from two-month peak as inflation-led rally loses steam","__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eAug 14 (Reuters) - Gold prices ‌steadied on Friday after pulling back from a more than two-month high, as investors booked profits following a rally fuelled by softer U.S. inflation data and fading expectations for a ​Federal Reserve rate hike in September.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eSpot gold was little changed at $4,351.45 an ​ounce by 1113 GMT after the contract fell 1.3% in the ⁠previous session. U.S. gold futures for December delivery fell 0.3% to $4,407.70 per ounce.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eBullion ​rallied to its highest point since June 5 on Thursday.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"Gold is barely holding ​onto a weekly advance, as markets indulge in some profit-taking following bullion's mid-week spike to a two-month high,\" said Han Tan, chief market analyst at Bybit.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"The coming week's Federal Open Market ​Committee meeting minutes may yet trigger fresh volatility for spot gold, even ​as traders keep a watchful eye on global oil benchmarks.\"\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eU.S. producer prices were unchanged in July, following ‌a ⁠revised 0.1% drop in June, while U.S. consumer prices barely increased last month as the cost of gasoline declined for a second consecutive month.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eTraders are now pricing only a 31% chance of a rate hike in September, down from about 44% ​a week earlier, according ​to the CME ⁠FedWatch Tool. FEDWATCH\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eLower interest rates boost gold as it does not yield interest.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003ePhysical gold discounts in India widened this week to ​their highest levels in more than two months as rallying ​prices curbed ⁠consumption, while interest in China remained muted.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eMeanwhile, oil prices climbed after Washington threatened an open-ended naval blockade on Iran, reviving supply concerns.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIn other metals, spot silver rose 0.3% ⁠to $64.66 per ​ounce. Platinum was up 0.4% to $1,724.90 per ounce ​and palladium gained 0.2% to $1,309.28 per ounce, both metals were headed for a weekly drop.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eReporting by ​Dharna Bafna and Swati Verma in Bengaluru; Editing by Devika Syamnath and Joyjeet Das\u003c/p\u003e","category":null,"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-08-14/gold-steadies-two-month-peak-inflation-led-rally-loses-steam","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-08-14T08:44:02-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T08:48:37-0400","imageUrl":null,"featured":true,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/a38de551-3f2a-4d0d-b3a0-9fa10f80323c.webp?top=0\u0026left=0.14542149511479\u0026width=1279.7091570098\u0026height=720\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=5d1ee083e70318345e436775ca1917a2620fef24289675f9ca3c9b51ba74265e"}}},"audioTts":null,"teaserSnippet":"Aug 14 (Reuters) - Gold prices ‌steadied on Friday after pulling back from a more than two-month high, as investors booked profits following a rally fuelled by softer U.S. inflation data and fading expectations for a ​Federal Reserve rate hike in September.Spot gold was little changed at $4,351.45 an ​ounce by 1113 GMT after the contract fell 1.3% in the ⁠previous session. U.S. gold futures for December delivery fell 0.3% to $4,407.70 per ounce.Bullion ​rallied to its highest point since June 5 on Thursday."},{"id":231335,"title":"Wall St Week Ahead: With Fed mum on next move, investors look to earnings to keep stocks afloat","__typename":"OffTheWire","body":"\u003cp\u003eNEW YORK, Aug 14 (Reuters) - Investors head into next week weighing familiar concerns over interest rates, inflation and geopolitical tensions against another robust earnings season ​that continues to keep stocks supported.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eWith little fresh guidance from policymakers and uncertainty brewing over the U.S. Federal Reserve's next move, many investors are increasingly ‌looking to corporate earnings — fueled in part by spending on AI infrastructure — to validate bullish assumptions and offset macroeconomic risks.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"The primary thing is going to continue to be the Federal Reserve,\" said Shawn Snyder, economic strategist at Potomac Fund Management. He sees the August 27 to 29 Jackson Hole symposium as the next major opportunity for policymakers to provide clarity on how they are interpreting inflation and growth.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"If ​you can't look to the Fed for guidance, then you have to increasingly look to earnings for guidance.\"\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eNEEDING REASSURANCE\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eFor investors like Snyder, the focus is less ​on a specific rate signal and more on whether policymakers can articulate a credible framework for navigating inflation and growth after bouts ⁠of mixed messaging.\u003cbr\u003e\u003cbr\u003eThat uncertainty has kept markets closely focused on real Treasury yields — returns adjusted for inflation — a key driver of asset prices. Rising yields can pressure technology ​stocks and other long-duration assets by increasing financing costs.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe dynamic is particularly important for AI-related companies and hyperscalers whose shares have been among the market's biggest drivers this year.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe S\u0026amp;P ​500 (.SPX), opens new tab hit a record high on Thursday as technology shares climbed and falling oil prices boosted risk appetite after a softer-than-expected U.S. producer price report. Treasury yields also fell as markets pared back rate-hike expectations next month.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eStocks and the S\u0026amp;P 500 information technology index (.SPLRCT), opens new tab have notched modest gains this week.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eEven so, investors remain alert to signs inflation could reaccelerate. Elevated energy prices and stalled U.S.-Iran talks have raised concerns ​that oil markets could jolt higher, potentially adding pressure to consumer prices and bond yields.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eFor now, however, strong corporate results have helped contain volatility. About 85% of S\u0026amp;P ​500 companies reporting have beaten earnings estimates, with profits up 32.7% excluding mark-to-market gains at Alphabet and Amazon, according to LSEG data.\u003cbr\u003eEarnings results from Walmart and chipmaker Analog Devices will offer ‌fresh clues into ⁠the health of the U.S. consumer and broader economy.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"Not only have we seen earnings beats and revenue beats so far, we're also seeing a lot of upside guidance as well and limited downside guidance from companies. They're the ones telling us, 'hey, the party is still on',\" said Andy Pratt, director of investment strategy at Burney Company. \"When you look at what are the things driving inflation right now, they're ... one-time shocks rather than something more persistent.\"\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eEarnings strength has been especially notable among companies tied to AI-related infrastructure spending, a ​theme investors expect to remain front and ​center in coming weeks.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e'EARNINGS TRUMP ALL ⁠OTHER PROBLEMS'\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eFor Chris Grisanti, chief market strategist at MAI Capital Management, hyperscaler spending plans are among the most important developments to watch. Recent earnings reports, he said, have increasingly clarified which companies stand to benefit most from the AI boom.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eBig Tech's AI spending ​is set to rise past $700 billion this year from $400 billion in 2025, and the scale of those investments has fueled ​questions about whether hyperscalers can ⁠generate sufficient returns to justify the massive capital outlay.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eYet, Grisanti dismissed concerns that those companies are overextending themselves financially, arguing their balance sheets remain among the strongest in corporate America and that aggressive investment reflects strong demand.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"If you were running that business, you would beg, borrow, and steal to get more money to put into that business,\" he said, referring ⁠to some of ​the fast-growing cloud businesses.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eStill, investors acknowledge that oil prices and geopolitical developments could yet complicate the ​inflation and interest rate outlook. But after another earnings season marked by upbeat guidance and heavy AI investment, many see corporate America as the strongest current defense against those risks.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\"It's a nice time to be an ​equity investor because earnings trump all the other problems,\" said Grisanti. \"I think that will become clear as the second half (unfolds).\"\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eReporting by Laura Matthews in New York Editing by Rod Nickel\u003c/p\u003e","category":null,"urlAlias":"/news/off-the-wire/2026-08-14/wall-st-week-ahead-fed-mum-next-move-investors-look-earnings-keep","source":{"name":"Reuters","subtitle":null,"description":null},"createdAt":"2026-08-14T08:33:10-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T08:41:16-0400","imageUrl":null,"featured":true,"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null,"image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/a8c61b15-b8e3-48e2-a4b5-09fed9190dc1.webp?top=0\u0026left=0.14542149511479\u0026width=1279.7091570098\u0026height=720\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=91764e6e67d095dd339b340181578c3f7d64f136591fdbbda6caf237d9c9446c"}}},"audioTts":null,"teaserSnippet":"NEW YORK, Aug 14 (Reuters) - Investors head into next week weighing familiar concerns over interest rates, inflation and geopolitical tensions against another robust earnings season ​that continues to keep stocks supported.With little fresh guidance from policymakers and uncertainty brewing over the U.S. Federal Reserve's next move, many investors are increasingly ‌looking to corporate earnings — fueled in part by spending on AI infrastructure — to validate bullish assumptions and offset macroeconomic risks."}]},"cacheTagsHeader":null},"dataUpdateCount":1,"dataUpdatedAt":1786832947394,"error":null,"errorUpdateCount":0,"errorUpdatedAt":0,"fetchFailureCount":0,"fetchFailureReason":null,"fetchMeta":null,"isInvalidated":false,"status":"success","fetchStatus":"idle"},"queryKey":["newsLandingPage"],"queryHash":"[\"newsLandingPage\"]"},{"dehydratedAt":1786832947394,"state":{"data":{"nodeListTrending":[{"id":230956,"__typename":"NewsArticle","category":{"id":48,"name":"Gold","urlAlias":"/news/category/commodities/gold"},"teaserSnippet":"(Kitco News) – Gold prices are benefiting from renewed momentum on lower rate hike expectations and strong central bank demand, while India’s new import controls drive domestic demand even lower, according to precious metals analysts at Heraeus.In their latest update, the analysts noted that gold prices posted their best week since January on hopes for a deal to reopen Hormuz.\u0026nbsp;","title":"Gold prices soar on rate optimism and sovereign buying, India’s import controls sap silver demand – Heraeus","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/article/2026-08-10/gold-prices-soar-rate-optimism-and-sovereign-buying-indias-import-controls","source":{"name":"Kitco News","subtitle":"The Leading News Source in Precious Metals","description":"\u003cp\u003eKitco NEWS has a diverse team of journalists reporting on the economy, stock markets, commodities, cryptocurrencies, mining and metals with accuracy and objectivity. Our goal is to help people make informed market decisions through in-depth reporting, daily market roundups, interviews with prominent industry figures, comprehensive coverage (often exclusive) of important industry events and analyses of market-affecting developments.\u003c/p\u003e\r\n"},"audioTts":null,"createdAt":"2026-08-10T09:48:08-0400","updatedAt":"2026-08-10T09:58:47-0400","image":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/1ab7d722-8804-40da-a20c-8dd36003aa92.png?top=0.15981657608696\u0026left=0\u0026width=1672\u0026height=940.68036684783\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=c4fbd8f8920ec5b2aa0cc541efca67823792faa3cd5b3ff1354f55199d2e5167"},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/1ab7d722-8804-40da-a20c-8dd36003aa92.png?top=0.15981657608696\u0026left=0\u0026width=1672\u0026height=940.68036684783\u0026scale_width=200\u0026token=356452318289021e5bd7c62c0148e9a60b39f69daadbfc0dec30ff71b03d3e91","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/1ab7d722-8804-40da-a20c-8dd36003aa92.png?top=0.15981657608696\u0026left=0\u0026width=1672\u0026height=940.68036684783\u0026scale_width=400\u0026token=ea4c1c95ad42dc2e68de6afdfeee1be474655f83862554fd7c20788b932b73b5","media":"all and (min-width: 0px)"},"desktop":{"media":"all and (min-width: 1024px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/1ab7d722-8804-40da-a20c-8dd36003aa92.png?top=0.15981657608696\u0026left=0\u0026width=1672\u0026height=940.68036684783\u0026scale_width=1920\u0026token=c4fbd8f8920ec5b2aa0cc541efca67823792faa3cd5b3ff1354f55199d2e5167"},"mobile":{"media":"all and (min-width: 0px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/1ab7d722-8804-40da-a20c-8dd36003aa92.png?top=0\u0026left=365.59021406728\u0026width=940.81957186544\u0026height=941\u0026scale_width=768\u0026token=1caf4c562100b7a039096b2de2422753bb0faaab6a906405f754a29b0b88d94a"},"tablet":{"media":"all and (min-width: 768px)","srcset":"https://image-api.prod.kitco.com/image/1ab7d722-8804-40da-a20c-8dd36003aa92.png?top=0.15981657608696\u0026left=0\u0026width=1672\u0026height=940.68036684783\u0026scale_width=1024\u0026token=c7123a925c2ced947d403b63481d3246edf15f488b47b6499f257542819f6bee"}}}},"teaserImage":{"detail":{"default":{"srcset":null},"sources":{"teaser_small":{"srcset":null,"media":null},"teaser_medium":{"srcset":null,"media":null},"desktop":{"media":null,"srcset":null},"mobile":{"media":null,"srcset":null},"tablet":{"media":null,"srcset":null}}}},"legacyThumbnailImageUrl":null},{"id":230964,"__typename":"NewsArticle","category":{"id":48,"name":"Gold","urlAlias":"/news/category/commodities/gold"},"teaserSnippet":"(Kitco News) – Gold’s nearly $300 breakout rally last week is generating renewed momentum in the market, and even if prices need more time to digest this latest move, one fund manager said the precious metal has a clear path back to $5,000 an ounce.\u0026nbsp;","title":"Gold could reclaim $5,000/oz in two years, prices could gain 9% in 2026 - Catalyst Funds’ Miller","teaserHeadline":null,"urlAlias":"/news/article/2026-08-10/gold-could-reclaim-5000oz-two-years-prices-could-gain-9-2026-catalyst-funds","source":{"name":"Kitco News","subtitle":"The Leading News Source in Precious Metals","description":"\u003cp\u003eKitco NEWS has a diverse team of journalists reporting on the economy, stock markets, commodities, cryptocurrencies, mining and metals with accuracy and objectivity. Our goal is to help people make informed market decisions through in-depth reporting, daily market roundups, interviews with prominent industry figures, compr